From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bsp2020 <bsp2020@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:09:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/36] moe cache: KV segment -- one take per request
 (GGML_MOE_KV_SEG_TAKE1)

With the KV segment the lend line follows the cells in use, so a long
prompt takes rows back from the expert cache once per step of the line:
each take synchronises every lending device, rewrites the cold pointers
of the copies it drops and zeroes the rows (~45 ms a time).

The server now tells the lending cache, before each batch, how many
cells the prompts in progress still need (this batch plus what is left
of every slot in PROCESSING_PROMPT): llama_kv_lend_hint().  The cache
keeps that as a floor under lend_need(), so the first batch of a request
takes everything the prompt will reach in one take.  The hint is sent
every update_slots iteration, 0 when no prompt is in progress, so a
cancelled request corrects itself.

Only a hint: every per-ubatch lend_need() call stays as it was, so a low
or missing hint degrades to the stepwise takes and a high one only drops
copies early.  The copies are duplicates, so the output does not change.
Parked slots still take every row.

Default on with GGML_MOE_KV_LEND=1 and GGML_MOE_KV_SEG=1, inert
otherwise; GGML_MOE_KV_SEG_TAKE1=0 restores the stepwise takes.

The take log line (GGML_MOE_KV_LEND_LOG) also splits its time into the
first synchronise, the pointer writes, the memset and the second
synchronise, to show where a take spends it.
